\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 Chapter Three\
\pard\tx566\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\cf0 	It broke a little after midnight. The first gust took the slates off the chapel roof and sent them skating down the lane like playing cards.\
\cf0 	The gulls had gone inland days ago. Nobody in the village said what that meant, though everybody knew it, the way you know the weight of a coat you have worn all winter.\
\cf0 	Down at the slip the old men sat on upturned crates and talked about other years: the year the ice came into the harbour, the year the herring failed, the year the lifeboat went out and came back with no one in it.\
\cf0 	She walked the length of the sea wall and back again, and then once more, because walking was something to do with her hands and her feet while her head went over the same ground it had been over all morning.\
\cf0 	Hal was at the door before she had her boots on. \'93The boat,\'94 he said, and she understood him: the last boat, the one they had kept for the road.\
\cf0 	They ran.\
